  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find ${L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ for $F(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}-1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}-1}=\\frac{2}{(s-1)(s+1)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-1},\\,s>1 \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+1},\\,s>-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}-1}$ is $f(t)={e}^{t}-{e}^{-t}.$",
